      ],
      "level": 1,
      "text": "3. Hexadecimal, including fractions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "d1b0f8ea-4d27-528a-9135-0b8121793262",
      "citations": [
        {
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1,
          "chunk_id": "7e4c9ea0-9451-4ac0-8b7e-6f03ba35783a"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1,
          "chunk_id": "7e86764a-353e-4b9a-9c2b-0aeaeabedf63"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1,
          "chunk_id": "306c2d70-d7a0-4134-bb40-4d841072b4a5"
        }
      ],
      "text": "Each hexadecimal digit corresponds to exactly four bits because $16=2^4$. A–F represent decimal 10–15.\n\nStart at the radix point and group outward. Add leading zeroes on the integer side or trailing zeroes on the fraction side; never insert padding next to the radix point."
    },
